/**
 * ---- SearchCodingSpan: every classification at one c. span, for the *_INF benign/VUS arms --------
 * The positional census `DescribeVariant` cannot give. `DescribeVariantResponse.classified_in_gene` is
 * classification-scoped — pathogenic only, by the search term — so it supports "no P/LP record at
 * this codon" and never "no informative variant at this codon", which is the question the trees ask:
 * every *_INF rule scores a B/LB informative variant (-2.0 first B, -1.0 first LB) and a VUS one
 * (0.0) alongside its pathogenic arm.
 *
 * THE SPAN, NOT THE GENE, IS THE UNIT, because that is what the rules key on: SM19's *_INF codes ask
 * about the same CODON (MIS_INF) or the same EXON (NUL_INF, SPL_INF), never about the gene. A
 * gene-wide all-classification pool would be wrong-shaped twice over — it answers a question no rule
 * asks, and where it matters most it is unfetchable (LDLR alone carries 2304 pathogenic records at
 * floor 0, before any other classification), so a bound would cut it to a prefix that says nothing
 * about any one codon. Span-scoped, the census is complete and small: 3 records at SCN2A codon 370,
 * 17 across a 51-nt TRDN exon (measured, August 2026).
 *
 * HOW THE SPAN IS SEARCHED, since it decides what lands in it. ClinVar's Entrez index has no c.
 * coordinate to search on — `einfo` lists `[Base Position]` and no transcript-relative field — so the
 * c. range is projected through the transcript's own exon table onto genomic coordinates and the
 * search runs on those, scoped to the gene the table names. That index is GRCh38, which is why the
 * rpc carries no genome build and pins GRCh38: the exon table has to be read on the assembly the
 * search is indexed by, and the RECORDS are the same set on either build anyway.
 *
 * `[Base Position]` holds a record's two ENDPOINTS and not the bases between them (measured: a 6-nt
 * deletion answers a search at its first and last base and at neither base in between), so a
 * coordinate range alone would miss precisely the record that CONTAINS the span — a deletion
 * beginning before a codon and ending after it, which is an informative variant at that codon — and
 * miss it as an empty census. A second clause therefore searches a 1000-nt-wider window for records
 * at least as long as the span, and every hit is then measured against the span, so what comes back
 * is the records that genuinely meet it. The residual, stated because no clause reaches it: a record
 * longer than that window and extending past it on BOTH sides — a copy-number record, whose extent
 * no c. range expresses and whose per-exon reading is `Transcript.AssessExonRelevance`'s.
 *
 * Three further consequences the caller reads the result under: a record whose title is written on a
 * DIFFERENT transcript of the gene is IN (its `coding_span.transcript` says so, and its c. numbers
 * are that transcript's, not the requested one's); a c. range crossing an exon boundary maps to one
 * genomic interval that spans the intervening intron, so the census is then a superset of the exonic
 * bases asked about (`searched_span` is what actually ran, and each record's
 * `coding_span.intron_offset` places it); and a record ClinVar annotates to a neighbouring gene
 * alone is OUT.
 *
 * Doc maps fields to codes:
 *   records            -> every record in the span, of EVERY germline classification, each with its
 *                         `classification`, `review_stars`, `review_status`, `conditions` and
 *                         `coding_span`: the *_INF candidate set at that position, both arms.
 *                         ELIGIBILITY stays the model's (SM19) — `submissions` is empty here as it
 *                         is on `classified_in_gene`, so a candidate that has to be weighed is
 *                         re-asked of `DescribeVariant` under its own accession. A record ClinVar
 *                         carries with no germline classification at all is present with an empty
 *                         `classification` rather than dropped: it is in the span, and it is not an
 *                         informative variant. Usually that is a NoClassification record — ClinVar's
 *                         fourth classification kind, an evidence_only submission — and less often a
 *                         somatic- or oncogenicity-only one.
 *   total_in_span / considered_in_span / span_truncated -> the census, read as DescribeVariantResponse's.
 * An EMPTY `records` is the finding — most codons carry no ClinVar record — and it comes back as an
 * empty list. It is checked apart from the one other thing that produces an empty search: a symbol
 * ClinVar files nothing under, where the exon table and ClinVar disagree about the gene's name and
 * the search would be empty at any coordinates. That is this service's own inconsistency to
 * reconcile, not an answer, so it fails the rpc rather than arriving as the finding.
 *
 * @generated from message themis.rpc.clinvar.SearchCodingSpanRequest
 */
export type SearchCodingSpanRequest = Message<"themis.rpc.clinvar.SearchCodingSpanRequest"> & {
  /**
   * Versioned RefSeq coding accession (required), e.g. "NM_001042492.3" — the transcript the c.
   * coordinates below are numbered on and whose exon table projects them, exactly as
   * GetStructureRequest takes it. An unversioned accession, an Ensembl ENST, an `NR_` or a
   * full HGVS is INVALID_ARGUMENT.
   *
   * @generated from field: string transcript = 1;
   */
  transcript: string;

  /**
   * The span's first and last c. coordinate on `transcript`, both REQUIRED and both inclusive:
   * positive = in the CDS (c.3496), negative = 5'UTR (c.-20), and 0 is INVALID_ARGUMENT because c.
   * numbering has no 0 — which is also how an unset field is caught. `cds_end` must not precede
   * `cds_start`. A codon is its three bases (1108, 1110); an exon is that exon's `cds_start`/`cds_end`
   * from Transcript.GetStructure.
   *
   * The offset forms have no integer encoding, exactly as on GetStructureRequest: a 3'UTR
   * position (c.*n) and an intronic one (c.3496+1, c.3497-2) cannot be given, so a wholly-3'UTR exon
   * cannot be asked about this way. Records AT those positions still land in the span whenever the
   * genomic interval reaches them. A signed int rather than an HGVS string is what leaves those forms
   * unrepresentable instead of runtime-rejected, and it is the encoding GetStructureRequest takes.
   *
   * @generated from field: int32 cds_start = 2;
   */
  cdsStart: number;

  /**
   * @generated from field: int32 cds_end = 3;
   */
  cdsEnd: number;

  /**
   * How many matching records to fetch and summarise. REQUIRED — 0 is INVALID_ARGUMENT, and so is a
   * value above 500. Lower than DescribeVariantRequest.max_pool_records because the other leg is different:
   * the exon table comes from VariantValidator, which self-extends to 60 s, so what is left of the
   * rpc's own budget is one esummary page and not four. The bound is a guard rather than a lever
   * either way — a codon or an exon holds tens of records, not thousands, so `span_truncated` set
   * says the span is wider than a rule's unit.
   *
   * @generated from field: int32 max_records = 4;
   */
  maxRecords: number;
};

